# Exercise: Reaching Deeper with dip
#
# `dip` hides exactly one value while running a quotation. To
# reach *further* into the stack, you rearrange inside the
# quotation. The cleanest shape is:
#
#   x [ swap ...work on the new top... swap ] dip
#
# That hides x with `dip`, the inner `swap` brings the next item
# up, the body operates on it, the trailing `swap` restores
# order, and `dip` puts x back on top when the quotation
# returns.
#
# (You might be tempted to *nest* dips — `[ [ quot ] dip ] dip`.
# The type checker can't prove the inner dip has a value below
# its quotation, because inside a quotation the surrounding
# stack is row-polymorphic. Use the swap-inside form instead.)
#
# Your task: given stack ( 100 5 3 ), add 50 to the 100 (the
# deepest of the three values), preserving both 5 and 3.
# Result: ( 150 5 3 ).
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-dip-deeper ( -- )
    100 5 3
    # Your code here — hide 3 with dip, swap inside to expose
    # 100, add 50, swap back.
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Top should be 3
    3 test.assert-eq
    # Second should be 5
    5 test.assert-eq
    # Third should be 150
    150 test.assert-eq
;
